# Order Intelligence Layer — local environment template.
#
# Copy to `.env` (which is gitignored) and fill in real values:
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is loaded on import by ai_service/settings.py (python-dotenv), and only
# fills variables NOT already set in the real environment — so shell exports and
# container/CI env vars still take precedence. Never commit a real `.env`.

# --- Azure AI Foundry — leave blank to run in LLM-fallback mode ---------------
# With these unset/blank, the AI service still runs: explanations/routing/
# summaries take the fallback path (source="fallback"). Fill them to enable the
# real LLM (source="ai") — no code change needed.
#
# Model recommendation (Azure AI Foundry, OpenAI GPT-5 family):
#   * EXPLAINER + ROUTER -> gpt-5.4-nano  (lightest/cheapest, low-latency; these
#     are the high-frequency calls — 2 per WARN/ERROR log. Nano is plenty: the
#     router output is enum-validated in nodes.py, so a small model is safe.)
#   * SUMMARY            -> gpt-5.4-mini  (runs rarely — once per completed
#     journey — so the small step up in capability costs almost nothing.)
#   Simplest option: gpt-5.4-nano for all three. (nano/mini need no registration;
#   full gpt-5 does.) Set the values below to the DEPLOYMENT names in your Foundry
#   resource (a deployment name is chosen at deploy time — not the model id).

# ENDPOINT: paste either the Foundry project endpoint (".../api/projects/<name>")
#   or the OpenAI-compatible service URL (".../openai/v1"). The code normalizes a
#   project endpoint to the /openai/v1 form, which is what accepts the API key
#   below (the raw project endpoint requires Entra ID auth, not a key).
#AZURE_AI_FOUNDRY_ENDPOINT=
#AZURE_AI_FOUNDRY_API_KEY=
# API_VERSION: leave as "preview" (the GA v1 rolling version). A dated value like
#   2024-12-01-preview is rejected with 400 "API version not supported".
#AZURE_AI_FOUNDRY_API_VERSION=preview
#AZURE_AI_FOUNDRY_DEPLOYMENT_EXPLAINER=
#AZURE_AI_FOUNDRY_DEPLOYMENT_ROUTER=
#AZURE_AI_FOUNDRY_DEPLOYMENT_SUMMARY=

# --- Infrastructure (defaults match docker-compose.yml; override if needed) ---
# ES_URL=http://localhost:9200
# REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/0
# RABBITMQ_URL=amqp://guest:guest@localhost:5672/
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://oil:oil@localhost:5432/oil

# --- Full-Docker run (`docker compose --profile app up`) ----------------------
# Only needed to run the whole stack in containers (see CLAUDE.md "Running").
# NPM_TOKEN: a GitHub Packages read token — REQUIRED to build the dashboard
#   image (it installs the private @computacenter-ro/style-guide package). The
#   backend/Python services do NOT need it. Without it, `--profile app up` fails
#   at the dashboard build step only.
# NPM_TOKEN=
# POSTGRES_USER / _PASSWORD / _DB default to oil/oil/oil (compose); keep in sync
#   with DATABASE_URL if you change them.
# POSTGRES_USER=oil
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=oil
# POSTGRES_DB=oil

# --- Poller / journey tuning (optional; shown with their defaults) ------------
# POLL_INTERVAL=10
# WINDOW_START_OFFSET=25
# WINDOW_END_OFFSET=5
# STALLED_TIMEOUT=90

# --- Production deployment (docker-compose.prod.yml only) ---------------------
# Used ONLY by the production compose file on the Azure VM. Ignored in local dev.
#
# PUBLIC_HOST: the public DNS name of the deployment, WITHOUT scheme
#   (e.g. oil.example.com). REQUIRED — compose refuses to start without it.
#   Caddy requests a Let's Encrypt certificate for exactly this name, so it must
#   already resolve to the VM and ports 80/443 must be reachable.
# ACME_EMAIL: address Let's Encrypt uses for expiry warnings. Recommended.
# PUBLIC_HOST=
# ACME_EMAIL=
#
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD is REQUIRED in production (no "oil" default there).
#
# INJECT_INTERVAL / INJECT_COUNT: the continuous injector's cadence — fire
#   INJECT_COUNT scenarios together, then wait INJECT_INTERVAL seconds.
#   Defaults give 2 scenarios per minute. A container restart resets the cycle
#   to the first pair.
# INJECT_INTERVAL=60
# INJECT_COUNT=2
#
# --- Prebuilt images (docker-compose.registry.yml) ---
# Only needed when deploying with the registry override, i.e. pulling the images
# built by .github/workflows/build-images.yml instead of building on the VM.
#
# IMAGE_OWNER: the GHCR owner, LOWERCASE (GHCR rejects uppercase paths).
# IMAGE_TAG:   which build to run. A branch name tracks that branch's latest —
#   convenient, but it MOVES the next time the workflow runs, so a `pull` can
#   swap the image mid-week. Pin a `sha-<full-sha>` tag for the demo.
# IMAGE_OWNER=computacenter-ro
# IMAGE_TAG=prep-for-prod

# --- Production auth notes (see the Auth section for the vars themselves) -----
# In the production compose these two are set for you, derived from PUBLIC_HOST —
# override here only if you must:
#
#   PASSWORD_LOGIN_ENABLED=false
#     Entra is the deployment's sign-in path. The app default is `true` (for local
#     dev) and the built-in admin hash is bcrypt("admin"), so leaving password
#     login on in a deployment is a way around SSO. Flip it back to `true`
#     temporarily if the Entra client secret expires mid-demo.
#
#   ENTRA_REDIRECT_URI=https://<PUBLIC_HOST>/auth/entra/callback
#     Must match the Azure app registration BYTE-FOR-BYTE, and Entra requires
#     HTTPS for non-localhost redirect URIs. Note this path has NO /api prefix:
#     Microsoft redirects the browser straight to it, which is why the Caddyfile
#     routes /auth/entra/* to the backend separately from /api/*.
#
# Access control is NOT enforced in code — any tenant account that signs in gets
# in. Restrict it in the Azure portal (Enterprise Application → "Assignment
# required" + user/group assignment); no code change needed.

# --- Collector store cap (pipeline/mock_es; optional) -------------------------
# MOCK_ES_MAX_LOGS: ring-buffer capacity of the in-memory log store. Oldest logs
#   are evicted once full, which is safe because nothing at runtime reads old
#   logs (the poller only asks for a ~20s window). Bounds memory on a long
#   continuous run — at ~2 flows/30s the injector emits ~170k lines/day, so an
#   uncapped store would grow without limit. GET /health reports stored/capacity.
# MOCK_ES_MAX_LOGS=200000

# --- Teams webhooks (backend [5]; unset => cards print to stdout) -------------
# TEAMS_WEBHOOK_NETWORKING=
# TEAMS_WEBHOOK_DEVOPS=
# TEAMS_WEBHOOK_BACKEND=
# TEAMS_WEBHOOK_DATABASE=
# TEAMS_WEBHOOK_GENERAL=

# --- Auth (backend [5]; Phase 1: single hardcoded admin user) -----------------
# The dashboard requires login; the backend REST API + /ws require a valid
# session cookie. Defaults let you log in as admin/admin for local dev only.
#
# ADMIN_USERNAME: the one login name.
# ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH: bcrypt hash of the password. Generate one with:
#   python -c "from backend.auth import hash_password; print(hash_password('your-pw'))"
#   (Leave unset to use the built-in admin/admin default — dev ONLY.)
# JWT_SECRET: HMAC secret signing the session token. MUST be set (>=32 bytes)
#   for any real deployment; the dev default is guessable and logs a warning.
# JWT_TTL_SECONDS: session lifetime (default 28800 = 8h).
# AUTH_COOKIE_SECURE: "true" to send the cookie only over HTTPS. Keep false for
#   local http://localhost; set true behind TLS.
# ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
# ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=
# JWT_SECRET=
# JWT_TTL_SECONDS=28800
# AUTH_COOKIE_SECURE=false
